\name{proteins_parse_accessions}
\alias{proteins_parse_accessions}
\title{Read accessions into entry, isoform or version, and applied variants, with mzLib}
\usage{
proteins_parse_accessions(accessions, timeout = NULL)
}
\arguments{
\item{accessions}{A character vector, one accession per result row, in order, duplicates
  included. Sent on stdin, unchanged: not trimmed, not split. A blank or \code{NA} entry, or one with a
  line break, is refused, because it would shift every later row.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} (the default) to wait.
}

}
\description{
Read protein accessions back into their entry, isoform or version, and applied sequence variants, with mzLib's accession grammars, never repairing one that does not fit.

Answers **which entry, isoform and variants does \code{P04637-2_S70N} or \code{NP_000537.3_R72P} name?**
Wraps mzLib's \code{VariantApplication.ParseAccession} (mzLib #1382), and through it
\code{ProteinAccession.Parse}. It is the inverse of the names mzLib gives variant proteoforms:
\code{P04637_S70N} is entry \code{P04637} with \code{S70N} applied, and \code{NP_000537.3_R72P} is RefSeq
\code{NP_000537}, version 3, with \code{R72P}. Use it to link a search result's accession to anything you
keep per entry - genes, orthologs, GO terms - without losing the isoform or the variants.
Cutting at the first \code{_} would turn \code{NP_000537} into \code{NP}.
}
\details{
\strong{It parses and never repairs.} An accession outside UniProt's and RefSeq's protein grammars
comes back \code{"unrecognized"} and verbatim - lower case, a stray space, a FASTA header, a \code{DECOY_}
prefix, a \code{|}-joined protein group, and \code{P12345_2}, mzLib's loader counter for a *different*
entry whose accession collided. mzLibR sends each accession exactly as you give it.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_parsed_accessions}. \code{record_count} counts the accessions parsed, duplicates
  included. \code{namespace_counts} is a named vector, namespace to the number of accessions in it,
  every namespace present (\code{uniprot}, \code{refseq}, \code{unrecognized}), zeros included: a large
  \code{unrecognized} count is worth reading, see \code{caveats}. \code{with_applied_variants_count} counts the
  accessions that name applied variants. \code{caveats} says what mzLib leaves unrecognized rather
  than repairs.

  \code{records} is a data.frame with one row per accession, in the order given: \code{input}, exactly as
  given; \code{entry_accession}, the UniProt entry or unversioned RefSeq accession (\code{P04637} for
  \code{P04637-2_S70N}), equal to \code{input} when unrecognized - an \strong{entry}, not a sequence;
  \code{isoform}, the UniProt \code{-n} number, \code{NA} without one (always for RefSeq) or past 2,147,483,647;
  \code{version}, the RefSeq \code{.n} number, \code{NA} without one (always for UniProt) or past
  2,147,483,647; \code{namespace}; \code{parent_accession}, the entry the variants were applied to,
  isoform or version kept (\code{P04637-2} for \code{P04637-2_Q5*}), equal to \code{input} without variants;
  \code{applied_variants}, the suffix as written (\code{S70N_A80T}), \code{NA} without one; and
  \code{has_applied_variants}.

}
\section{Joining on it}{


\code{entry_accession} answers "same entry?", \strong{not} "same sequence?": \code{P04637} and \code{P04637-2} share
it, but they are different sequences. Join per-entry data on it, and sequence-level data on
\code{input}. \code{namespace} is spelled as \code{\link{proteins_resolve_genes}} spells it, so the two tables join.
\code{setNames(parsed$records$entry_accession, parsed$records$input)} maps each accession to its
entry, for grouping.

}
